<TopicPage slug="subjects/math/expert/complex-analysis">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Complex analysis is calculus on functions of a complex variable — inputs and outputs in the
    plane of a + bi. Its miracle: demanding that a function be differentiable in the complex
    sense is <strong>so restrictive that everything becomes rigid and beautiful</strong>.
    Complex-differentiable functions are infinitely smooth, determined by their values on any
    tiny patch, and obey integral formulas that feel like magic until the rigidity sinks in.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="Euler's formula, derived honestly — the series substitution that married exponentials to rotation" widget="equationStep" config={euler2Cfg} />

  <h2>The pillars</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Analyticity:</strong> complex-differentiable on a region means the function has a power series everywhere there. One derivative implies all of them — impossible in real calculus.</li>
    <li><strong>Cauchy's integral theorem:</strong> the integral of an analytic function around any closed loop is zero — the plane's holes are the only thing that matters, and residues measure them.</li>
    <li><strong>Residues compute real integrals:</strong> nasty real integrals fall to loop integrals in the complex plane. This trick evaluates Fourier transforms and the propagators of quantum field theory.</li>
    <li><strong>Conformal maps:</strong> analytic functions preserve angles locally — turning hard geometries (flow around a wing) into easy ones (flow around a circle).</li>
    <li><strong>The Riemann hypothesis:</strong> the deepest unproven conjecture in mathematics lives here — a statement about where the zeros of the zeta function sit, equivalent to sharp control over how primes are distributed (the number-theory lesson's prime counting).</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Physicists use complex numbers daily because waves and rotations <em>are</em> the same
    operation (Euler's formula) — complex analysis is less an extension of calculus than the
    language waves prefer to speak.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Needham, Visual Complex Analysis; Ahlfors, Complex Analysis.</p>
</TopicPage>
